  [3mComplexity:[0m trivial.

  [1mB. A bare storage helper, no tracking concept.[0m Add two functions that read and write a persisted ID, and let each form
  [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m whatever it wants alongside it.
  [3mTrade-offs:[0m smallest possible diff and nothing speculative. But "other forms will need it later" means every one of
  them re-invents the logging shape, and when you do add a real analytics backend there's no single place to change. It
  defers the abstraction to the point where it's expensive.
  [3mComplexity:[0m trivial.

  [1mC. Convert the browser code to ES modules.[0m Add [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m, make the identity store a real module, import it where
  needed.
  [3mTrade-offs:[0m the conventional answer for an app expecting to grow, with real encapsulation instead of a global. Costs:
  breaks [38;5;153mfile://[39m loading, so you'd need a local server to develop; and [38;5;153msrc/[39m is CommonJS, so the repo ends up with two
  module systems. That's a bigger change to the app's shape than your request implies.
  [3mComplexity:[0m moderate.

  I recommend [1mA[0m. It gives you the one thing B lacks — a single place where "track" is defined — while staying inside the
  app's existing no-build, classic-script shape, which C would change out from under you. If the app later grows enough
  to need modules, A's four functions port into a module unchanged.

  One thing I'll write into the design regardless of which you pick: the persisted ID is for labeling logs only. Anyone
  can edit it in devtools, so no endpoint may ever treat it as proof of who's asking — that decision stays server-side.
